WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.920
Superhero movies might be the biggest thing
at the box office today, and with the kind

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.529
of epic action that we’re getting from the
Marvel Cinematic Universe, it’s easy to

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:11.959
see why. That hasn’t always been the case,
though. The history of the genre is full of

00:00:11.959 --> 00:00:16.581
catastrophic failures that never managed to
save the day for audiences or critics. From

00:00:16.581 --> 00:00:21.019
obscure heroes who were a little too bizarre
to household names that had a rough road to

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.400
the screen, here are the worst superhero movies
ever made.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:25.960
Barb Wire

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.179
Barb Wire might not be a traditional superhero,
but with her over-the-top costume, dystopian

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:35.160
sci-fi setting, and roots on the page, she
seemed like the perfect comic book character

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.950
to make Pamela Anderson an action star…
in theory. In practice, the 1996 adaptation

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.990
didn’t give the fans or the cast anything
they wanted, except maybe seeing Anderson

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:48.700
in a leather costume that the Telegraph called
"unnecessarily revealing."

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:54.540
Set in the distant future of 2017, the movie
starred Anderson as Barb Wire, a bartender

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:59.140
and bounty hunter trying to escape the Second
American Civil War by dealing with a mobster

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.900
named Big Fatso and making enough money to
move to Canada. With a premise like that,

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.571
it’s easy to see why Anderson thought she
was signing on for what she called "a dark

00:01:07.571 --> 00:01:12.691
comedy," about a blonde, big-haired badass
riding around on a motorcycle. With the studio

00:01:12.691 --> 00:01:17.119
demanding changes to the script, a new director,
and less humor, however, what could've been

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.900
a campy classic was tanked from the start.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.540
Howard the Duck

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.990
You might've been excited to see Howard the
Duck pop up in the post-credits scene in Guardians

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:30.030
of the Galaxy, but don’t let your interest
in one of Marvel’s more obscure characters

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:34.840
trick you into watching his first feature
film outing from 1986. Produced by George

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:40.050
Lucas — yes, that George Lucas — the movie
starred Back to the Future’s Lea Thompson,

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:45.070
giving it a sci-fi pedigree that any movie
in the '80s would've been jealous of. Unfortunately,

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:48.650
while Universal was trying to get the guy
who made Star Wars, they got the guy who made

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:50.120
The Phantom Menace instead.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.000
“What I don’t know is what the hell I’m
doing here.”

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.860
After Robin Williams wisely passed on the
role, the job of voicing Howard went to theater

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.530
actor Chip Zien, who knew something was wrong
when he had to buy his own ticket to see the

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:07.979
movie he starred in. As he later said in an
interview, by the time they’d gotten through

00:02:07.979 --> 00:02:13.329
111 minutes of a duck puppet’s questionable
romance with Marty McFly’s mom, he and his

00:02:13.329 --> 00:02:16.780
friend Roy were alone in the theater. Everyone
else had bailed.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.040
“Howard you really are the worst.”

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:21.340
Green Lantern

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.900
With roles in Blade Trinity and Deadpool,
Ryan Reynolds has had decent luck with superhero

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:32.349
movies. His attempt at bringing DC’s Green
Lantern to life in 2011, however, risked ruining

00:02:32.349 --> 00:02:37.840
his career and taking the whole superhero
genre with it. After it cost a whopping $300

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.510
million to make — about 80 million more
than it took in — the scathing reviews and

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:46.670
terrible box office numbers threatened to
turn studios off the idea of comic book movies

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:47.440
altogether.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:53.280
Fortunately for Reynolds and the entire superhero
genre, the same weekend Green Lantern opened,

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.519
he shot the now-famous Deadpool test footage
that would convince Fox to give him a shot

00:02:57.519 --> 00:03:02.730
at redemption. Reynolds took the chance, and
unlike Green Lantern, Deadpool was an R-Rated

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:03.380
smash

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:04.940
Steel

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:10.800
Black Panther brought superheroics to Africa
and moviegoers into theaters in record-breaking

00:03:10.809 --> 00:03:16.129
numbers, and Blade kicked off the modern superhero
film, but neither King T'Challa nor the Daywalker

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:20.329
were the first black comic book hero to make
it to the big screen. It’s often overlooked,

00:03:20.329 --> 00:03:24.609
but Shaquille O'Neal beat them both to the
punch when he starred in the 1997 adaptation

00:03:24.609 --> 00:03:29.739
of DC's Steel… although you can’t really
blame anyone for forgetting about this downright

00:03:29.739 --> 00:03:35.150
awful movie. It was so bad that director Kenneth
Johnson apologized for it, admitting that

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:39.020
he probably should have walked away once he
found out who his star was going to be.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.819
Shaq definitely had the heroic proportions
to bring the Superman-inspired armored hero

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:49.010
to life, but lacked the acting chops to save
a miserable script that feels like a bad first

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:54.329
draft of Iron Man. As Johnson revealed, however,
Warner Bros. co-president Bill Gerber opted

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:58.449
for O'Neal over the likes of Wesley Snipes
and Denzel Washington because he thought Shaq

00:03:58.449 --> 00:04:02.299
would "sell more toys." Considering Gerber
was fired the next year, we’re guessing

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.740
the toy sales weren't much better than the
movie.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:05.980
Spawn

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:11.220
When you sit down to watch a DVD commentary
and the first thing the director says is "you

00:04:11.269 --> 00:04:17.180
can blame it all on me," that’s not a good
sign. That's exactly how Mark Dippè introduces

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:21.519
himself in the Spawn commentary. At the time,
he was known as a visual effects wizard, but

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:26.449
he'd never directed a movie before — and
it showed. When Den of Geek revisited the

00:04:26.449 --> 00:04:31.020
1997 film, they pointed out that for a movie
based entirely around its visuals, quote,

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:35.360
“a good number of the effects aren't good.
Some are flat out awful."

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:40.380
As a former staffer at Industrial Light and
Magic, the visual effects should’ve been

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.540
Dippè’s specialty, although as far as he
was concerned, the CGI wasn't the problem.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:50.480
Speaking to Wired in 1997, the outspoken filmmaker
lashed out at censors, claiming,

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:54.980
"The MPAA is driving a stake through my heart
with this, because they're making me take

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:56.520
this movie down so much."

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:01.260
He also called it "a very positive story,"
which is a little hard to believe considering

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:05.260
that it’s a movie about a zombie superhero
getting yelled at by a demon clown.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:06.520
Daredevil

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:11.960
Long before he donned the Dark Knight's cape
and cowl in the DC film universe, Ben Affleck

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:17.639
made his ill-fated foray into the superhero
genre with 2003's Daredevil. Despite Affleck’s

00:05:17.639 --> 00:05:21.870
genuine love for the character, the cinematic
version suffered from the tonal whiplash of

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:26.300
ultra-serious images mixed with goofy action
like the playground fight between Daredevil

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:30.970
and Elektra, and a murderous supervillain
whose menacing insanity came off as cartoonish

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.480
— and not in the good way.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.120
With a bad script and incredibly awkward visual
effects, it’s no surprise that critics offered

00:05:37.129 --> 00:05:41.910
up some unfavorable comparisons to Spider-Man,
which hit theaters the previous year. They

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:46.639
weren’t the only ones. Affleck himself called
Daredevil the only movie that he regrets making,

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:51.470
and agreed that the 2015 Netflix version was
doing the character far better than a movie

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:54.400
that, as he said, quote, "never kind of got
it right."

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.460
The Fantastic Four

00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:01.780
Technically, Roger Corman's The Fantastic
Four has never been released, but this low-budget

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.379
take on Marvel's first family became a thing
of legend after bootleg copies began making

00:06:06.379 --> 00:06:11.240
their way around the convention circuit. The
story goes that Neue Constantin Films contacted

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:16.410
the veteran B-movie producer after failing
to secure a $40 million partnership with 20th

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:21.340
Century Fox. Nobody else wanted in, so since
the rights to the Marvel property would expire

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:27.120
if a movie wasn’t in production by December
31, 1992, Corman was hired to make the cheapest

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.220
movie he could.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:34.180
“God help us!”

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:39.889
The result was a movie so legendarily bad
that it became the subject of a 2015 documentary

00:06:39.889 --> 00:06:44.740
called Doomed from director Marty Langford.
The saddest revelation in that film was that

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:49.080
the cast had no idea that they were just making
a movie to help secure the rights, and that

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:54.460
there were never any plans to actually release
it and give a cast of unknowns their big breaks.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:07:02.100
“Look at it! See it! Does it amuse you?”

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:05.800
You can’t blame them for being fooled, but
they probably should’ve noticed something

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:10.759
was up when they could barely hear Dr. Doom’s
dialogue over the clanking of his armor, or,

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:14.200
you know, when they saw what the Thing was
supposed to look like. To be fair, though,

00:07:14.389 --> 00:07:19.740
the origin story in this film is actually
more faithful to the comics than in any other

00:07:19.749 --> 00:07:24.509
Fantastic Four movie to date. Of course, the
fact that we still think this one is the worst,

00:07:24.509 --> 00:07:28.740
even after those other three, should give
you an idea of just how bad it is.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:30.300
Catwoman

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.139
If you watch Halle Berry's notoriously terrible
Catwoman movie and Christopher Nolan's game-changer

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:40.980
Batman Begins back to back, it's hard to believe
there was only a single year between them.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:47.360
This extremely loose DC adaptation was nominated
in seven categories at the Razzies and triumphed,

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:52.169
sort of, in four, including Worst Actress
for Berry. She even showed up in person to

00:07:52.169 --> 00:07:56.830
collect the award along with co-star Alex
Borstein and her Academy Award, and started

00:07:56.830 --> 00:08:00.880
off by thanking the studio for casting her
in what she called “a godawful movie.”

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.860
And that was the nicest thing she said about
it in that speech.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.800
Rest assured, it actually is that bad. In
addition to scenes where Berry’s magical

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:12.979
cat powers kick in during a basketball game
and cause her to swat at the ball like a ball

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:19.159
of yarn, it’s full of cringeworthy stereotypes
for a female superhero. Seriously: Catwoman

00:08:19.159 --> 00:08:24.819
fights a villain whose big plan is to distribute
evil makeup, because she’s mad that she’s

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:25.680
starting to look old.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:30.660
In 2018, Berry addressed Catwoman once again
while accepting the Matrix Award from New

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:35.150
York Women in Communication. She acknowledged
how bad the picture was, but she defended

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:39.230
her decision to accept the role, revealing
that the, quote, "sh--load of money" she made

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:42.700
changed her life and that the movie taught
her “what not to do.”

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:44.480
Captain America

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:50.240
After ten years of the Marvel Cinematic Universe,
it's hard to imagine anyone but Chris Evans

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:55.580
as Captain America. Back in 1990, though,
Matt Salinger, son of famed American author

00:08:55.580 --> 00:09:00.710
J. D. Salinger — was tossing shields when
Evans was still in elementary school. Originally

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:05.350
intended to be released on the 50th anniversary
of Captain America’s first appearance, this

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:10.250
low-budget Marvel adaptation was never actually
finished, with the production running aground

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:14.750
before the final scenes were shot. Unwilling
to send the existing footage to the deep,

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:19.100
dark vault that it most certainly belonged
in, studio execs had editors cobble together

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.500
a version that could be released on VHS.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:25.280
“Mr. President! Thanks!”

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:30.810
Salinger later revealed that the film was
as grueling to shoot as it is to watch, with

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:35.510
his rubber suit causing him to overheat. On
top of that, the costume looked ridiculous,

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:39.390
thanks largely to the addition of rubber ears
attached to the helmet, and the movie’s

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:44.060
Red Skull was inexplicably portrayed as an
Italian mobster, complete with a hilariously

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:45.240
over the top accent.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:54.900
“That was a gun … THIS is a detonator.
Did you really expect me to be taken alive?!”

00:09:55.220 --> 00:10:00.000
As for the script, well, there’s a scene
where Cap grabs the Red Skull by the wrist

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:05.740
and the Skull pulls out a knife... and chops
off his own hand. Not exactly the evil mastermind

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:06.780
fans were expecting.

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:08.860
Condorman

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:15.400
Dubbed an "ideological intervention from the
Disney machine" by Time Out, 1981's Condorman

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:20.900
is a camp cocktail of Cold War spy thriller,
comedy and superhero movie, all watered down

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:25.680
for family consumption. This misfire from
the Mouse House stars Michael Crawford as

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.260
Woody Wilkins, a comic book creator who insists
on doing everything he writes in real life,

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:35.200
just to prove it can be done. After accidentally
getting involved with a seductive Soviet spy,

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:38.460
Wilkins has to become the hero he created,
flying and all.

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:43.530
For the most part, Crawford himself was in
the ghastly Condorman suit — and at one

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:48.630
point, he almost died in it. After falling
into a river in costume, the gigantic wings

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:53.600
dragged him down and nearly drowned him, making
the film unpleasant on both sides of the camera.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:58.280
That said, it’s been more than 35 years
since Condorman hit theaters. Maybe it’s

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:03.380
time to introduce Disney’s first attempt
at a superhero to the MCU… or not.

